const e=JSON.parse(`[{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Exposed to harsh environmental conditions for centuries, lighthouses such as La Jument lighthouse off the coast of Brittany, France, exemplify the ______ between marine structures and ocean forces: the continuous impact of breaking waves necessitates periodic reinforcements to or redesigns of the structures that in turn may alter local wave patterns.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"continuum"},{"name":"B","content":"tension"},{"name":"C","content":"incongruity"},{"name":"D","content":"interplay"}],"correct":"interplay","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p> ______ the mythological and heroic subject matter often found in works by Ary Scheffer and other establishment figures, Honoré Daumier's painting <i>The Third-Class Carriage</i> instead focuses on something decidedly mundane: working-class travelers in a third-class railway car.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"Eschewing"},{"name":"B","content":"Expounding"},{"name":"C","content":"Caricaturing"},{"name":"D","content":"Appraising"}],"correct":"Eschewing","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Though copies of <i>The Adventures of Indiana Jones in Wenceslas Square in Prague on January 16, 1989</i>—an underground computer game that was created anonymously in 1989 as an act of political protest against the authoritarian regime of what was then Czechoslovakia—were originally distributed ______ , the game is now readily available online for anyone to play.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"succinctly"},{"name":"B","content":"surreptitiously"},{"name":"C","content":"disingenuously"},{"name":"D","content":"dispassionately"}],"correct":"surreptitiously","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>In their study of caterpillars in fragmented forests (formerly contiguous forests that have been broken into isolated patches by roads or other interruptions), Nicole M. Dallar et al. stress that although fragmentation inevitably ______ alterations to local ecological processes, the changes will likely have a greater impact on <i>Parallelia bistriaris</i>, a specialist feeding on a limited number of plant species, than on a species like <i>Morrisonia confusa</i> that feeds on several.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"attenuates"},{"name":"B","content":"preempts"},{"name":"C","content":"subsumes"},{"name":"D","content":"engenders"}],"correct":"engenders","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Typically, synthetic leather is petroleum based, but materials scientists searching for an ecologically sustainable alternative have used various bacteria that secrete linear chains of glucose, forming a dense mesh of cellulose called a pellicle, which is leather-like except in color. The standard process for dyeing leather generates substantial wastewater and other undesirable byproducts, so <u>adopting such a regimen would run counter to the ecological promise of the pellicle approach.</u> To address this, Kenneth T. Walker and colleagues worked to modify <i>Komagataeibacter rhaeticus</i> bacteria to produce a pellicle with embedded pigmentation cells, thereby allowing the pellicle to \\"dye\\" itself from the inside.</p>","question":"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"To describe a consideration that led the researchers to employ an alternative approach to coloring a pellicle that allowed them to achieve their main goal as presented in the text."},{"name":"B","content":"To indicate the characteristic of conventional synthetic leathers that makes those leathers poorly suited to achieve the researchers' main goal as presented in the text."},{"name":"C","content":"To illustrate how the researchers adapted the pellicle approach to overcome a potential impediment to their main goal as presented in the text."},{"name":"D","content":"To concede that the researchers' main goal as described in the text will be challenging to achieve due to the standard coloring approach being impractical for use on a pellicle."}],"correct":"To describe a consideration that led the researchers to employ an alternative approach to coloring a pellicle that allowed them to achieve their main goal as presented in the text.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p><strong>Text 1</strong><br>Good art often challenges and disrupts social and aesthetic norms, but the creation of public art—paintings, sculptures, and performance pieces displayed in nonmuseum or nontheatrical public settings—typically requires broad agreement among artists, civic officials, and community members about the works' message and artistic goals. <u>Public art that fails to appease everyone by being sufficiently aesthetically and conceptually bland almost inevitably provokes backlash.</u><br><br><strong>Text 2</strong><br>Public art is commonly displayed in spaces intended for purposes other than meaningful aesthetic engagement. Some critics of public art therefore note that norm-defying pieces that aren't effectively integrated within their surroundings in a manner that primes passersby to appreciate the pieces' merits (as is often the case) tend to be regarded more unfavorably than similarly provocative art encountered in museums is.</p>","question":"Based on the texts, how would the critics mentioned in Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"By contending that the kinds of reactions controversial public artworks often receive aren't exclusively the result of attributes inherent in the works themselves."},{"name":"B","content":"By disputing the notion that civic leaders and community members are easily placated by art that is intended mainly to reinforce social norms."},{"name":"C","content":"By agreeing with the idea that only works of art that are universally appealing are suitable for displaying in public spaces."},{"name":"D","content":"By arguing that the reason members of the general public might disagree about a public artwork's merits is unrelated to the unconventionality of its appearance and ideas."}],"correct":"By contending that the kinds of reactions controversial public artworks often receive aren't exclusively the result of attributes inherent in the works themselves.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>The following text is from Ralph Waldo Emerson's 1841 essay <i>The Method of Nature</i>.</p><blockquote style=\\"margin-left: 40px;\\">The scholars are the priests of that thought which establishes the foundations of the earth. No matter what is their special work or profession, they stand for the spiritual interest of the world, and it is a common calamity if they neglect their post in a country where the material interest is so predominant as it is in America.</blockquote>","question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"In a country whose citizens are largely preoccupied with tangible gains, it is crucial that some people work to foster and preserve ideas."},{"name":"B","content":"The habit of intellectuals of privileging ideas over material things invariably leads to tragedy."},{"name":"C","content":"It is important for Americans of all kinds to have at least some military experience if society is to function properly."},{"name":"D","content":"Scholars have played an important role in society for a much longer time than have those people who are mainly concerned with financial gain."}],"correct":"In a country whose citizens are largely preoccupied with tangible gains, it is crucial that some people work to foster and preserve ideas.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Ian Gilligan et al. claim that during the Ice Age, humans at Varvarina Gora in Siberia made fitted clothing by boring holes in pieces of hide and fur with a sharpened bone tool called an awl and then threading cord through the holes to bind the pieces together. Eventually, as evidenced by artifacts at Aitzbitarte III in Europe, someone perforated the end of a bone awl to make an eye (hole) to hold the cord, thereby creating a sewing needle with which both steps could be performed in a single motion. This sped up the process of making clothing and, by freeing them from the need to push cord through holes manually, allowed artisans to make smaller holes and thus finer seams.</p>","question":"Based on the text, what can be concluded about clothing made during the Ice Age?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"Clothing made with awls at sites like Varvarina Gora likely had finer seams than clothing made with needles at sites like Aitzbitarte III did."},{"name":"B","content":"Clothing made with awls at sites like Varvarina Gora could likely be made more quickly than clothing made with needles at sites like Aitzbitarte III."},{"name":"C","content":"Clothing made with awls at sites like Varvarina Gora likely had coarser seams than clothing made with needles at sites like Aitzbitarte III did."},{"name":"D","content":"Clothing made at sites like Varvarina Gora was likely made with needles, while clothing made at sites like Aitzbitarte III was likely made with awls."}],"correct":"Clothing made with awls at sites like Varvarina Gora likely had coarser seams than clothing made with needles at sites like Aitzbitarte III did.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Uwe Fritz and colleagues' 2014 investigation, which examined the evolutionary trajectory of the turtle species <i>Pelomedusa subrufa</i>, demonstrates the potential held by museomics — the study of hDNA, genomic data from archival specimens housed in collections such as those at the Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History. Although it is a challenge for evolutionary biologists that museum specimens have been prone to exogenous DNA contamination, emerging advances in DNA extraction and sequencing may soon enable hDNA to reveal a range of evolutionary relationships and patterns.</p>","question":"What does the text most strongly suggest about museomics?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"By expanding the types of DNA samples available to researchers, it is becoming a widely used alternative to traditional genomic methods."},{"name":"B","content":"It is an approach to the study of evolutionary phenomena that holds much promise for future scientific advances."},{"name":"C","content":"As a result of recent technological breakthroughs, it is no longer a challenging approach for evolutionary biologists to use."},{"name":"D","content":"It can provide researchers access to DNA from reptiles, but it requires the latest and most advanced laboratory equipment."}],"correct":"It is an approach to the study of evolutionary phenomena that holds much promise for future scientific advances.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>From glass production to fuel for residential heating, hydrogen has many applications. Currently producing hydrogen is carbon intensive, but Alexandra M. Oliveira is one of many scholars investigating the feasibility of large-scale production of hydrogen through electrolysis, a process that emits no carbon when renewable energy is used. Oliveira suggests that although some obstacles may prevent electrolytic hydrogen from dominating energy systems, there is utility in its ability to decarbonize the glass and heating industries, where reducing carbon emissions is especially challenging.</p>","question":"Which statement about the use of hydrogen in the glass and heating industries is most strongly supported by the text?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"The adoption of electrolytic hydrogen by these industries has been impeded by concerns about how carbon intensive its production is."},{"name":"B","content":"At least some of electrolytic hydrogen's potential to lessen these industries' carbon emissions is as yet unrealized."},{"name":"C","content":"Development of applications for electrolytic hydrogen is less advanced in these industries than it is in most other carbon-intensive industries."},{"name":"D","content":"The fact that large-scale electrolytic hydrogen production is not yet available has limited these industries' interest in pursuing hydrogen-based applications."}],"correct":"At least some of electrolytic hydrogen's potential to lessen these industries' carbon emissions is as yet unrealized.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p style=\\"text-align: center;\\"><img src=\\"/images/3c32f90ffd601a5dc871f0bccff4da52.png\\" style=\\"width: 700px; height: 286px;\\"></p><p>A Pew Research Center survey conducted in January 2024 found that three out of ten US adults make at least one New Year's resolution (a promise for the year ahead), while half of those who make a resolution make more than one. The survey asked participants what kinds of resolutions they made and separated them into several categories. The table presents percentages of people who make particular kinds of New Year's resolutions among those who choose to make them, indexed by age bracket.</p>","question":"Which choice best presents a conclusion about the habits of New Year's resolution makers that is best supported by information in the text and the table?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"The majority of US adults who make resolutions related to health and exercise also make resolutions in multiple additional categories."},{"name":"B","content":"Among all US adults, people become less likely to make New Year's resolutions as they age, regardless of the type of resolution."},{"name":"C","content":"Resolution makers between the ages of 18 and 29 are more likely to make resolutions about health and exercise than resolution makers between the ages of 30 and 49 are."},{"name":"D","content":"Resolution makers between the ages of 50 and 64 are more likely to make resolutions related to personal relationships and less likely to make resolutions related to finances than resolution makers between the ages of 30 and 49 are."}],"correct":"Resolution makers between the ages of 50 and 64 are more likely to make resolutions related to personal relationships and less likely to make resolutions related to finances than resolution makers between the ages of 30 and 49 are.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p><i>Richard II</i> is a play from the 1590s by William Shakespeare. Although King Richard has been vanquished by his cousin Henry Bolingbroke, he intimates that he is not entirely ready to show subservience to his cousin, saying, ______ </p>","question":"Which quotation from <i>Richard II</i> most effectively illustrates the claim?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"\\"I have no name, no title, — / No, not that name was given me at the font, — / But 'tis usurp'd: — Alack the heavy day, / That I have worn so many winters out, / And know not now what name to call myself!\\""},{"name":"B","content":"\\"Am I both priest and clerk? Well then, amen. / God save the King! although I be not he; / And yet, amen, if heaven do think him me.\\""},{"name":"C","content":"\\"Still my griefs are mine. / You may my glories and my state depose, / But not my griefs; still am king of those.\\""},{"name":"D","content":"\\"Alack, why am I sent for to a king, / Before I have shook off the regal thoughts / Wherewith I reign'd? I hardly yet have learn'd / To insinuate, flatter, bow, and bend my knee.\\""}],"correct":"\\"Alack, why am I sent for to a king, / Before I have shook off the regal thoughts / Wherewith I reign'd? I hardly yet have learn'd / To insinuate, flatter, bow, and bend my knee.\\"","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<div style=\\"text-align: center;\\"><img height=\\"347\\" src=\\"/images/a6f49fde61e11fcc5195c6490b2bc6ec.png\\" width=\\"942\\"></div><p>A marine biologist is researching four scleractinian coral species, which are also called stony corals because of the hard skeletons they develop. Consulting the table, she notes that the two species for which the maximum depths are most similar are ______</p>","question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"<i>Danafungia scruposa</i> and <i>Scolymia lacera</i>."},{"name":"B","content":"<i>Astreopora expansa</i> and <i>Scolymia lacera</i>."},{"name":"C","content":"<i>Astreopora expansa</i> and <i>Acropora echinata</i>."},{"name":"D","content":"<i>Acropora echinata</i> and <i>Danafungia scruposa</i>."}],"correct":"<i>Acropora echinata</i> and <i>Danafungia scruposa</i>.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Interested in how the color of dogs' irises affects human responses to dogs, Akitsugu Konno et al. showed images of 12 dogs' faces to human participants and asked them to rate the dogs' kindness and confidence as well as the likelihood that they would interact with or keep the dogs. The researchers had previously adjusted the images so that each dog was presented in two versions, one with light irises and one with dark irises. They distributed the images such that no participant saw both the light and dark versions of the same dog. Konno et al. found that participants responded more positively to the latter. The study's design allowed the researchers to exclude the possibility that the results ______ </p>","question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"were a function of participants emphasizing the colors of the dogs' irises over the dogs' kindness and confidence when reacting to the images."},{"name":"B","content":"reflected a preference on the part of participants for facial features that happened to coincide with dark irises in the dogs depicted in the images."},{"name":"C","content":"could be explained by participants having preexisting positive feelings about dogs with dark irises or preexisting negative feelings about dogs with light irises."},{"name":"D","content":"may have been different if participants had been able to see the dogs' irises in person instead of only seeing images that included the dogs' irises."}],"correct":"reflected a preference on the part of participants for facial features that happened to coincide with dark irises in the dogs depicted in the images.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Duckweed is a small freshwater plant that is often exposed to zinc pollution. Sofia Vámos and colleagues collected samples of four duckweed ecotypes (genetically and geographically distinct populations within a species), along with water from each ecotype's habitat. Hypothesizing that each ecotype is adapted to its local conditions in ways that bolster its growth and resistance to pollutants, the researchers grew each ecotype in all four water samples and with three levels of zinc (none, low, high). (The researchers did not replicate local differences in light or temperature.) They found that the ecotypes grew equally well in all four water samples and that adding zinc consistently enhanced growth, regardless of concentration, suggesting that ______</p>","question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"while the ecotypes are genetically and geographically distinct, those differences do not represent adaptations to local environmental conditions."},{"name":"B","content":"if each ecotype is indeed locally adapted as the researchers hypothesized, those adaptations are to other environmental conditions than the water each ecotype inhabits."},{"name":"C","content":"there may not be significant differences in the water that each ecotype inhabits, but there are significant differences in each ecotype's resistance to zinc pollution."},{"name":"D","content":"although the researchers' hypothesis does not appear to be supported, this may be because the levels of zinc exposure the plants in the experiment received did not match their exposure in their natural environments."}],"correct":"if each ecotype is indeed locally adapted as the researchers hypothesized, those adaptations are to other environmental conditions than the water each ecotype inhabits.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>While some biologists cite the Darwinian species concept (Jolly 2014), others adhere to the evolutionary species concept (Barraclough 2019), with at least fourteen other concepts used across the field. Such lack of consensus, however, isn't necessarily cause for alarm. Scholars supporting a pluralist approach—in place of a single, monistic concept—to defining a species ______ that a diversity of species concepts fits their equally diverse applications throughout the discipline.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"to argue"},{"name":"B","content":"is arguing"},{"name":"C","content":"argue"},{"name":"D","content":"argues"}],"correct":"argue","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Growing as large as three meters in diameter, the leaves of the giant Amazonian waterlily feature a complex network of radiating veins that provide structural ______ in thickness from the center to the edges, these veins allow the leaves to maintain their large size and buoyancy with minimal material, optimizing light capture and photosynthesis.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"support decreasing"},{"name":"B","content":"support, while decreasing"},{"name":"C","content":"support, decreasing"},{"name":"D","content":"support. Decreasing"}],"correct":"support. Decreasing","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>During a 2022 expedition led by research zoologist Andrea Quattrini, scientists discovered a new species of black coral off the coast of Puerto Rico. Like other black corals, the newly discovered species, which they named <i>Aphanipathes puertoricoensis</i>, has coiled branching structures that ______ it survive in the deep sea.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"help"},{"name":"B","content":"has helped"},{"name":"C","content":"helping"},{"name":"D","content":"helps"}],"correct":"help","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>To British musician Jacob Collier, substituting the ii chord for the bVll chord—its opposite in pitch and tone—adds expression to improvisation without creating dissonance. This conceptualization of harmonic opposites did not originate with ______ in 1985, the idea was first articulated in Swiss musicologist Ernst Levy's posthumously published book <i>A Theory of Harmony</i>.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"Collier, though"},{"name":"B","content":"Collier; though,"},{"name":"C","content":"Collier, though"},{"name":"D","content":"Collier, though;"}],"correct":"Collier, though;","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>The statement \\"all giraffes have long necks\\" is scientific because it could be proved false by a single observation to the contrary, according to Karl Popper. Popper's theory ______ that scientific hypotheses must be refutable, termed the criterion of falsifiability, rejects the confirmationist position that uses verifiability as the standard for scientific hypotheses.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"was dictating"},{"name":"B","content":"dictating"},{"name":"C","content":"dictated"},{"name":"D","content":"dictates"}],"correct":"dictating","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Novelist and playwright Anna Maria Hall joined with 55 other prominent British writers in 1837 to petition the US Congress for greater copyright protections. This cadre of renowned ______ that American publishers' appropriation of their work caused, in the words of the petition, \\"deep and extensive injuries...on their reputation and property,\\" helped sow the seeds for the International Copyright Act of 1891.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"authors, asserting"},{"name":"B","content":"authors asserted"},{"name":"C","content":"authors, had asserted"},{"name":"D","content":"authors were asserting"}],"correct":"authors, asserting","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>In 2001, some 391 years after Galileo Galilei and Simon Marius independently discovered Jupiter's four largest moons (Io, Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto), scientists discovered the moon Sponde in orbit around Jupiter. ______ researchers have discovered eighty moons orbiting Jupiter.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"Rather,"},{"name":"B","content":"In other words,"},{"name":"C","content":"For example,"},{"name":"D","content":"To date,"}],"correct":"To date,","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>Brain imaging research led by neuroscientist Dwaynica Greaves found that actors showed suppressed responses in the left anterior prefrontal cortex (the portion of the brain associated with self-awareness) when their names were called during performances; ______ the actors’ responses were normal in nonacting contexts. These findings suggest that when embodying characters, performers may temporarily set aside their personal identities.</p>","question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"conversely,"},{"name":"B","content":"specifically,"},{"name":"C","content":"likewise,"},{"name":"D","content":"thus,"}],"correct":"conversely,","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><blockquote style=\\"margin-left: 40px;\\"><ul><li>Calida Garcia Rawles is an African American painter.</li><li>She is known for her large-scale, hyperrealistic paintings depicting African American figures in water.</li><li>The painting <i>Soar</i> (24 × 18 in) depicts a young girl in a white dress floating at the bottom left corner of the canvas.</li><li><i>Yesterday Called and Said We Were Together</i> (48 × 60 in) depicts two unidentifiable figures with their arms outstretched floating in the upper left of the canvas.</li><li>She paints the water with vivid blue colors, including midnight blue and aquamarine.</li><li>The mood in the paintings is relaxed.</li></ul></blockquote>","question":"Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize a difference between the two paintings?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"Calida Garcia Rawles captures the water in paintings such as <i>Soar</i> and <i>Yesterday Called and Said We Were Together</i> in vivid hues of midnight blue and aquamarine."},{"name":"B","content":"While some of Rawles's works, such as <i>Soar</i>, feature one figure in the composition, <i>Yesterday Called and Said We Were Together</i> features two figures floating in the vivid blue water."},{"name":"C","content":"Although the number of figures may differ, constant among Rawles's hyperrealistic works is the relaxed mood that the paintings evoke."},{"name":"D","content":"Among the many stunning works of Calida Garcia Rawles is <i>Yesterday Called and Said We Were Together</i>, a relaxed 48-by-60-inch painting depicting two unidentifiable figures floating with their arms outstretched in the vivid blue water."}],"correct":"While some of Rawles's works, such as <i>Soar</i>, feature one figure in the composition, <i>Yesterday Called and Said We Were Together</i> features two figures floating in the vivid blue water.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><blockquote style=\\"margin-left: 40px;\\"><li>Louis Ballard was a classical composer and citizen of the Quapaw Tribe.</li><li>Ballard's composition <i>Desert Trilogy</i> was nominated for the Pulitzer Prize in music in 1971.</li><li>His composition <i>Cacéga Ayuwípi</i> incorporates a Ute eagle-bone whistle, a traditional Native instrument.</li><li>Ethnomusicologist Tara Browner claims that Ballard's compositions rely on elements originating within Native musical traditions.</li></blockquote>","question":"The student wants to support Browner's claim about Louis Ballard's compositions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"According to Browner, composer Louis Ballard, who wrote <i>Desert Trilogy</i>, relies on elements with origins in Native musical traditions."},{"name":"B","content":"Classical composer Louis Ballard, who wrote <i>Cacéga Ayuwípi</i>, was nominated for the Pulitzer Prize in music in 1971."},{"name":"C","content":"Browner posits that Louis Ballard's compositions rely on elements originating within Native musical traditions; <i>Cacéga Ayuwípi</i> affirms this assertion, incorporating a Ute eagle-bone whistle."},{"name":"D","content":"Browner claims that Louis Ballard's compositions, one of which was nominated for the Pulitzer Prize, rely on elements originating within Native musical traditions."}],"correct":"Browner posits that Louis Ballard's compositions rely on elements originating within Native musical traditions; <i>Cacéga Ayuwípi</i> affirms this assertion, incorporating a Ute eagle-bone whistle.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><blockquote style=\\"margin-left: 40px;\\"><li>The farm-size transition hypothesis predicts that economic pressures associated with modernization result in smaller farms amalgamating into larger-scale commercial farms.</li><li>Masters et al. (2013): The average farm size in Asia \\"already has or will soon begin to rise.\\"</li><li>Promkhambut et al. (2023) argue that small rice farms in Thailand have adopted modern farming methods without a significant scaling-up of farm size.</li><li>Promkhambut et al.: \\"The persistence of [small] rice farms [in Thailand] does not represent a 'failure' to modernize or a 'truncated' transition; it is a <i>response</i> to modernization.\\"</li></blockquote>","question":"The student wants to make and support a claim regarding the applicability of the farm-size transition hypothesis to Thailand. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"Although the farm-size transition hypothesis may be applicable to some countries in Asia, it is inconsistent with the development of rice farming in Thailand."},{"name":"B","content":"Masters et al. report that the average farm size \\"already has or will soon begin to rise\\" in Asia, a finding that is consistent with the farm-size transition hypothesis."},{"name":"C","content":"Taken together, the studies by Masters et al. and Promkhambut et al. suggest that rice farms in Thailand have responded to the economic pressures associated with modernization by expanding in size."},{"name":"D","content":"The predicted shift to large-scale commercial farming may not hold true for rice farms in Thailand, where, according to Promkhambut et al., rice farms have remained small as they've modernized."}],"correct":"The predicted shift to large-scale commercial farming may not hold true for rice farms in Thailand, where, according to Promkhambut et al., rice farms have remained small as they've modernized.","solution":""},{"type":"choice","article":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><blockquote style=\\"margin-left: 40px;\\"><ul><li>In the Electoral College system that determines the outcome of US presidential elections, the winner of Delaware's popular vote receives all 3 of Delaware's electoral votes.</li><li>A 2001 article written by law professors Akhil and Vikram Amar proposed that states could instead agree to award their electoral votes to the winner of the national popular vote.</li><li>The Amars' idea is the basis of the National Popular Vote Interstate Compact (NPVIC).</li><li>If states holding a majority of electoral college votes (270) adopt the NPVIC, the compact will take effect and adopters will award their electoral votes accordingly.</li><li>Delaware adopted the NPVIC in 2019.</li></ul></blockquote>","question":"The student wants to explain the significance of Delaware's NPVIC adoption. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":[{"name":"A","content":"An agreement among US states to award their electoral votes to the presidential candidate who wins the national popular vote was adopted by Delaware in 2019."},{"name":"B","content":"If the NPVIC takes effect, adopter Delaware will award all 3 of its electoral votes to the winner of the national, rather than the state's, popular vote."},{"name":"C","content":"Because it has adopted the NPVIC, Delaware will award its electoral votes to the winner of the state's popular vote once states holding a majority of electoral college votes (270) do the same."},{"name":"D","content":"According to the idea proposed by the Amars, the winner of Delaware's popular vote will receive all of the state's electoral votes if the NPVIC takes effect."}],"correct":"If the NPVIC takes effect, adopter Delaware will award all 3 of its electoral votes to the winner of the national, rather than the state's, popular vote.","solution":""}]`);export{e as default};
